WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.200 --> 00:00:06.110
At 10,000 phones, the
database sat at 93% CPU.

2
00:00:06.360 --> 00:00:09.515
Each request cost it 22
milliseconds of work.

3
00:00:09.915 --> 00:00:13.978
The easy fix was a bigger machine,
and I fixed the code instead.

4
00:00:14.228 --> 00:00:16.773
And the answers had to
stay exactly the same.

5
00:00:17.273 --> 00:00:21.813
I ranked the database's busiest
statements by their share of total time,

6
00:00:21.813 --> 00:00:23.681
not by their average speed.

7
00:00:23.931 --> 00:00:28.500
Search, Discover and feed shares came
out on top, with more than half of it.

8
00:00:28.900 --> 00:00:32.933
They now read a page's keys first,
then fetch only those rows.

9
00:00:33.183 --> 00:00:37.400
A search of one or two letters skips
the fuzzy-match index entirely.

10
00:00:37.650 --> 00:00:42.494
Likes, saves, shares, comments and
follows each run four fewer statements.

11
00:00:42.994 --> 00:00:44.214
Before merging,

12
00:00:44.214 --> 00:00:50.405
392 recorded responses from the old
code had to match the new code exactly.

13
00:00:50.655 --> 00:00:51.640
They all did.

14
00:00:52.040 --> 00:00:55.160
Then the same test ran
again, on the same machine.

15
00:00:55.460 --> 00:00:59.503
Database CPU fell from 93% to 44%.

16
00:00:59.753 --> 00:01:03.136
Work per request fell from
22 milliseconds to 10.

17
00:01:03.386 --> 00:01:04.786
For a free member,

18
00:01:04.786 --> 00:01:11.925
a search on a single letter went from
3,310 milliseconds of database time to 54.

19
00:01:12.325 --> 00:01:14.064
Same hardware, half the work.
